course: dictionary
lesson: 10554
title: Unblock: undoing a block
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word unblock is made of un- and block. The un- part means undo or reverse, so to unblock something is to undo a block: you clear something that was stuck, or you let someone back in after you stopped them.
explain: The first meaning is about things. A sink or drain blocked with hair and soap can be unblocked, so water can flow through again. The second meaning is about phones and apps. If you block someone, they cannot contact you, and if you unblock them, they can contact you again.
explain: Context tells you which meaning you need. «Sam blocks Jake over a laugh, feels silly next morning, and unblocks him.» No sink is stuck here. Sam stopped Jake from contacting him, then let Jake back in. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: "Sam lets Jake contact him again next morning" still makes sense.
fact: Unblock means to clear something so things can pass through again.
fact: On a phone, app or website, to unblock someone means to let them contact you or see you again.
fact: Common phrases are «unblock the sink», «unblock a number» and «unblock someone».
fact: Blocking is for people who keep hurting you, not for friends who upset you in a temper.
fact: If you are not sure whether someone is safe, ask a trusted adult before you unblock them.
exercise: «The road was blocked by fallen trees until the workers unblocked it.» What does unblocked mean here? | Painted in bright colours | Moved to a new town | Closed for the night forever | *Cleared so cars could pass again
exercise: Maya blocked a number that kept sending her nasty messages. Her teacher says she can unblock it if she wants. What does unblock a number mean? | To call that number to say hello | To delete every message on your phone | *To let that number contact you again | To change the password on your phone
exercise: «The plumber used a long wire to unblock the kitchen sink.» What is the plumber doing? | Cleaning the taps with a cloth | Moving the sink to another room | Filling the sink with water | *Clearing the sink so water can drain away
test: «The drain was full of leaves, so the workers had to unblock it.» Why did the workers unblock it? | *So water could flow through again | So the drain would be deeper | So the drain would be painted | So the leaves would grow bigger
test: Ella blocked a stranger who was rude to her online. Should she unblock them straight away? | *No, only unblock if she feels safe, and ask a trusted adult first | Yes, always at once, whatever they did | Yes, but only after she deletes her whole phone | No, because unblock means to break a phone
exam: Which word is the opposite of unblock, as in blocking someone on a phone? | *Block | Greet | Repeat | Clear
